					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1259135&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608002780" alt="Hornby" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1259135)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;" >Hornby</a></span> <span class="odate time_1340852367 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Jun 2012 02:59</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1492537">
									

<p>Most horrific? Not necessarily. The list should neither be considered to be in exact chronological order nor in exact descending order of &quot;horrific-ness&quot;. The reason [DATA EXPUNGED] (which in my own headcanon is Foundation-related) is last is because, like &quot;causing a Foundation containment breach&quot; it had been added as a recent addition to the list. The Foundation does terrible things, contains things that do worse things, and generally doesn't expunge data just because it's <em>ohmygodwhatisthatitissohorrific!</em>; it expunges things because it has a valid reason to do so. When I wrote this, I intended it as some Foundation-affiliated person was accused of doing <em>something</em> that would have cost them their respect in the organization, which they covered up either 1) because of the potential security breach that the <em>something</em> posed if knowledge of it spread within the Foundation, and/or 2) the Powers That Be at the Foundation realized that whatever this <em>something</em> the fellow was accused of doing he/she didn't actually do and this was an attempt to help protect that person's reputation.</p>
<p>In other words, the Foundation may not care (as much) about rape or murder or whatever, but the [DATA EXPUNGED] is something it <em>does</em> care about enough to redact. While that which is behind the [DATA EXPUNGED] most certainly would destroy the good standing of the accused, there is no guarantee you or I would find it more horrific than rape or murder or whatever.</p>
<p>&lt;/author's commentary&gt;</p>
<p>So, I didn't just unthinkingly follow the &quot;put [DATA EXPUNGED] at the end of the list&quot; trope, but you are of course entitled to both think and vote how you like.</p>

							</div>

								
					
