			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1492552">
									

<p>First of all, thanks for the compliment on the writing style and tone.</p>
<p>As far as the idea goes, it is something which threatens not your life or limb but your reputation. I, for one, am far more terrified of &quot;adult fears&quot; - things like being falsely accused and subsequently disgraced, or threats to my children (once I have children), or being out of work and unable to put food on the table, or any of these sorts of things - as compared to run of the mill &quot;scary monster things.&quot;</p>
<p>As to the [DATA EXPUNGED], I responded with my thoughts on that above (simulpost with this one), so I shan't rehash that here.</p>
<p>As far as the charge of magic, and the object's age, the Foundation may well have been aware of both the object and associated folklore for some time (note the comments in the article about the original beliefs that simple possession of the object was sufficient to cause its effect in the second paragraph of the description), there's no reason that the Foundation was necessarily able to obtain it until recently. This is a small gemstone that would fit in your hand. Nothing's to say it didn't spend decades in a shoebox in somebody's attic - I'm sure I'm hardly the only person to have a collection of assorted shiny rocks gathering dust that I got from various places as a kid. Taking the historical accounts of the stone (accounts which are implied by the article to exist in the middle ages and classical era) and then actually <em>finding the damn thing</em> might have taken the Foundation decades - and as a &quot;Safe&quot; object, probably not something as high on the acquisitions priority as something &quot;Keter&quot;. If this was in your closet and you'd not touched it in fifteen years since that time Bobby accused you of threatening to punch him back in kindergarten (or whatever might have ruined your kindergarten reputation), then how would the Foundation necessarily know that it was there?</p>
<p>Remember, SCP articles are generally executive summaries. It is not at all uncommon for there to be (never written) supplements, addenda, logs, and all that which are either mentioned or simply implied to exist without appearing on the wiki.</p>

