				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1259135&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608002780" alt="Hornby" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1259135)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;" >Hornby</a></span> <span class="odate time_1340908811 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Jun 2012 18:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1493201">
									

<p>The reason for the dual key lock is to prevent personnel from abusing this - you don't want someone actually committing a crime, then sneaking in to open the box and stick their hand in to make it look like they touched the damn thing, then using the security camera footage to suggest that any evidence that the crime they committed was just a result of SCP-1453's effects. By making it so that you need two personnel to open the safe, it (hopefully) would prevent such abuse - the Foundation wouldn't want staff treating the stone like a get-out-of-jail-free card.</p>
<p>So, strictly speaking, the lock is not to protect against SCP-1453's effects; it is to prevent the Foundation from unnecessarily having to sort through as many administrative hoops if one of the less scrupulous staff members is accused of misconduct. As the old saying goes: &quot;locks keep honest people honest.&quot;</p>
<p>Edit: Ah. Typo. Fixed.</p>

